EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - THRESHING FLOOR - EVENING #3#

Low sun. The evening wind. Amos winnows alone, tossing forkfuls of threshed barley into the air; the grain falls and the chaff blows away, gold in the light. Across the valley, other floors are working.

Asher comes up and takes the second fork. Amos looks at him.

Asher plants his feet exactly like Amos, squares his shoulders exactly like Amos, and grunts exactly like Amos.

ASHER
(as Amos)
There is barley still to thresh. I will be up before you. I always am.

The corner of Amos's mouth moves, almost a smile. It's gone.

AMOS
You have never held a fork right in your life.

ASHER
I held it right for a moment. You were not looking.

They toss together for a while. Then Asher stops.

ASHER
I am asking him for my portion. Tonight.

Amos stops. Chaff drifts between them.

ASHER
Come with me. Ask for yours. Beyond the Jordan the streets are lit all night, and no one counts the oil.

AMOS
At your age I had threshed ten summers. You have slept through all of yours.

Asher grins, and tosses a forkful high, as if it were a joke.

AMOS
A son receives his portion when he bears his father to the tomb. Not before.

The grin goes.

AMOS
He fed half this valley through the bad year. They will say his son buried him alive.

ASHER
He went out in the dark for one ewe and came back singing.

ASHER
Every head on this hill is counted. Mine too.

ASHER
I want a will of mine own. Under this roof, I have none.

AMOS
Then you wish him dead. Tell me I lie. Brother. Tell me.

Asher can't.

Amos turns back to the wind and throws his forkful high. The chaff goes away down the valley.

AMOS
Ask him with your own mouth. I will speak no word for you.

